Erase everything — including hidden and leftover partitions
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
For the case "the stick has 32 GB but only shows 4 GB" or "there is a partition
on it I cannot get rid of".

USAGE
  usb wipe /dev/sdb
  usb wipe /dev/sdb --full     also zero the WHOLE device (slow)
  usb -s wipe /dev/sdb         show only, run nothing

WHY THREE ERASE STEPS?
  1. wipefs -a  finds and removes all known signatures, even orphaned ones that
     no partition table points at anymore.
  2. Zero the first 16 MB  clears MBR boot code and the primary GPT header.
     An isohybrid image (every Linux ISO) writes exactly there.
  3. Zero the last 16 MB  removes the GPT BACKUP header.
     GPT keeps a second copy of the table at the end of the device.
     That is precisely why a deleted partitioning otherwise keeps coming back.

--full: WHEN?
  Only when the stick is given away or sold. It overwrites every byte and takes
  about ten minutes for 32 GB. Against forensic recovery on flash storage even
  this helps only so much — wear levelling keeps copies in blocks that dd never
  gets to see.

AFTERWARDS
  The stick has neither a partition table nor a filesystem, so it is unusable
  until 'usb format' runs. That is intended, not a failure.
